WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.503 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:06.172
-Welcome to "Summer
Corrections," you guys.

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:10.943
You can't bedazzle handcuffs.

00:00:10.943 --> 00:00:15.014
A BeDazzler punches
through fabric, not metal.

00:00:15.014 --> 00:00:18.551
So if you were...
off to prison

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:21.788
and that was the one thing
you were looking forward to...

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:24.357
yet another parade
a jackal has rained on.

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:26.225
[ Laughter ]

00:00:26.225 --> 00:00:30.696
We showed a picture
of Abraham Lincoln in a fedora.

00:00:30.696 --> 00:00:34.400
We said it was a fedora.
We were told it was a trilby...

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.169
also that Lincoln could
not have worn a fedora

00:00:37.169 --> 00:00:42.275
because the style did not
come into fashion until 1883,

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:44.944
when French actress
Sarah Bernhardt

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:48.581
wore one in a play
called "Fédora."

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:50.482
This is all helpful
for you to know

00:00:50.482 --> 00:00:54.554
if you're ever looking
for a fun conversation ender.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:57.757
[ Laughter ]

00:00:57.757 --> 00:00:59.893
"You guys talking about hats?"

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:00.960
[ Laughter ]

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.129
"Ugh, Gary."

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:11.537
I said that Koozie was
a portmanteau of beer and cozy.

00:01:11.537 --> 00:01:13.072
I misspoke, of course.

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:18.711
It is a portmanteau
of cool and cozy.

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:22.648
Beer plays no part in it.

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:24.951
Of course,
a part of a portmanteau

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:26.319
is known as "apartmanteau."

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:35.427
My favorite portmanteau
involving the word beer

00:01:35.427 --> 00:01:39.932
is the word for when you have
a beer with your fiancée,

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:42.435
which is, of course,
Kelly Rowland.

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:45.638
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.274 --> 00:01:52.612
Hey, guys. "Avatar" takes place
on a moon, not a planet.

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:54.814
I said it was a planet.
It's a moon.

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:57.483
And if you remember from the
film, to get to that moon,

00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:59.786
you have to enter a cryosleep

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:01.487
because the trip is so long.

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:02.888
And if you want to know
how long,

00:02:02.888 --> 00:02:04.124
in order to get there,

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:07.159
it takes almost
as long as it takes

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:08.962
to make "Avatar 2."

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:11.331
[ Laughter ]

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:14.867
What is the hold up with
"Avatar"?

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:17.971
I mean, what little Na'vi I know
I'm going to forget.

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:21.875
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:25.410
The moon's name is Pandora.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.413
And it's very hard place.

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:30.983
It's an almost unlivable
place for humans...

00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:32.919
because it's Pandora with ads.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:36.523
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.993
My in-laws have Pandora
with ads.

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:44.563
[ Laughter ]

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:46.466
One of the many draws.

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:51.304
[ Exhales sharply ]

00:02:53.338 --> 00:02:56.642
We mentioned that Sidney Powell
chugging -- excuse me --

00:02:56.642 --> 00:03:01.881
a Diet Dr Pepper during her
January 6th testimony was bad...

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:06.719
bad brand recognition for
Diet Dr Pepper.

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:09.656
Our joke was it was almost
as bad as that time

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:12.125
Mussolini was pictured
drinking a Fanta.

00:03:12.125 --> 00:03:15.194
And, well, we heard a lot
from you guys

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:16.896
about the history of Fanta.

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:20.166
And if you're a big fan,

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:22.635
don't run to the Wikipedia page.

00:03:24.436 --> 00:03:28.107
Let's just say Fanta's history
has a German element.

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:32.545
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:34.113
Which is never --
Which is never --

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:38.017
If you're a fan
of the genealogy shows,

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:42.254
it's never good when
a German element pops up.

00:03:42.254 --> 00:03:43.890
When Skip Gates said,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.159
"Now, your grandparents
were from Buenos Aires,

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:48.594
but did you know
they moved there from Berlin?"

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:51.631
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:58.136
Fanta originated in 1940
in Germany

00:03:58.136 --> 00:04:02.574
due to the American trade
embargo against Nazi Germany.

00:04:02.574 --> 00:04:05.845
They had to find a replacement.

00:04:05.845 --> 00:04:08.480
You know, because no matter what
you think about World War Two

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.584
and the different sides and
I don't want to relitigate it...

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:14.554
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.554 --> 00:04:16.889
...everybody loves soda pop.

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:18.591
[ Chuckles ]

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:20.259
Um...

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:21.794
This made me laugh a lot

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:24.263
because this is all
on the Wikipedia page.

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:29.068
You know, so now we've
established the Fanta history.

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:32.771
In February 2015, a 75th
anniversary version of Fanta

00:04:32.771 --> 00:04:36.108
was released in Germany
packaged in glass bottles.

00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:38.244
An associated television
ad referenced

00:04:38.244 --> 00:04:40.313
the history of the drink
and said the Coca-Cola Company

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:44.517
wanted to bring back "the
feeling of the good old times,"

00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:46.785
which was interpreted
by many to mean Nazi rule.

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:48.788
The ad was subsequently
replaced.

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:50.856
[ Laughter ]

00:04:50.856 --> 00:04:52.191
"Klaus!

00:04:53.592 --> 00:04:56.863
Klaus, get in here!"

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:00.633
[ German accent ]
"Based on your tone,

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:02.302
you've see the new ad."

00:05:03.802 --> 00:05:05.271
[ Normal voice ] "Klaus!"

00:05:09.042 --> 00:05:12.411
But people still like Fanta.

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:13.813
We got to --
Look, some stuff

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:17.349
came out of Nazi Germany
that had upside, right?

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:19.351
VW cars. Fanta.

00:05:19.351 --> 00:05:21.487
US space program.

00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:25.825
[ Laughter ]

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:28.260
Oh, I also was told
I'm pronouncing Fanta wrong.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:29.661
It's "Fahnta."

00:05:29.661 --> 00:05:32.999
So to whoever was offended
by my pronunciation,

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:34.967
apologies, mein fuhrer.

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:38.271
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.377 --> 00:05:47.780
This is a tran-- So again,
we just had a two-week hiatus.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.849
I spent it with my kids.
Lovely time with my kids.

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:53.920
And you'll see why
this is a transition,

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.487
because if I'm doing
any corrections

00:05:55.487 --> 00:05:58.290
over the last two weeks,
it's to children, right,

00:05:58.290 --> 00:05:59.591
things they get incorrect.

00:05:59.591 --> 00:06:04.263
And your job as a parent,
to correct them.

00:06:04.263 --> 00:06:06.866
So this is a --
this is a true story

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:08.167
and a true thing
that my son said.

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:10.836
We were talking about the fact

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:12.939
that -- this was to
our six-year-old Ashe --

00:06:12.939 --> 00:06:17.310
how lucky he is that he knows
all four of his grandparents

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:23.082
and how his grandmother never
met any of her grandparents.

00:06:23.082 --> 00:06:26.686
My wife's mother, her family,
her parents were

00:06:26.686 --> 00:06:28.353
Holocaust survivors.
This is a true story.

00:06:28.353 --> 00:06:31.557
So we were telling this
to my son, and he's curious.

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:34.593
You know, he's a six-year-old,
so he's curious and he's said,

00:06:34.593 --> 00:06:36.862
"How did her grandparents die?"

00:06:36.862 --> 00:06:40.065
And my wife said,
"Well, they actually all died

00:06:40.065 --> 00:06:43.702
at the same time during a war."

00:06:43.702 --> 00:06:46.739
And he said,
"Were they soldiers?"

00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:48.641
And my wife, very deftly,
I think, said,

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:53.111
"Not all people who die in a war
are soldiers."

00:06:53.111 --> 00:06:57.116
And then my six-year-old said,
"Um, were they drummers?"

00:06:57.116 --> 00:07:00.486
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.224 --> 00:07:06.958
I guess the question is
is it okay

00:07:06.958 --> 00:07:09.895
when kids are too young
to talk about the Holocaust yet,

00:07:09.895 --> 00:07:11.464
do you go,
"Yeah, they were drummers"?

00:07:17.436 --> 00:07:19.471
Rabies.

00:07:19.471 --> 00:07:21.407
There were some different
takes on rabies.

00:07:21.407 --> 00:07:23.709
We talked about rabies.

00:07:23.709 --> 00:07:25.911
Someone wrote,
"When I was eight years old" --

00:07:25.911 --> 00:07:30.049
We talked about whether or not
you can test for rabies.

00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:31.651
And someone wrote,

00:07:31.651 --> 00:07:34.921
"When I was eight years old,
I had to get shots for rabies

00:07:34.921 --> 00:07:38.858
due to being bitten
multiple times by mice

00:07:38.858 --> 00:07:41.760
my sister and I had found
in a sack of grain.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.730
14 shots, one every day
for two weeks, in the stomach."

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.065
That is awful and I'm sorry
you had to go through that

00:07:47.065 --> 00:07:49.234
as an eight-year-old.

00:07:49.234 --> 00:07:53.805
How do you get bitten
multiple times by the mice?

00:07:53.805 --> 00:07:55.641
At some point, you just got
to get your hand

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:57.943
out of the sack, right?

00:07:57.943 --> 00:07:59.612
I mean, I get it.

00:07:59.612 --> 00:08:02.714
I mean,
if I, as an eight-year-old,

00:08:02.714 --> 00:08:05.418
came across a sack of grain,
I'd be pretty excited, too.

00:08:05.418 --> 00:08:07.052
[ Laughter ]

00:08:07.052 --> 00:08:08.053
But at some point,
you got to just

00:08:08.053 --> 00:08:09.589
let the mice have their grain.

00:08:15.127 --> 00:08:17.296
But then somebody else said --
hold on real quick --

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:19.965
there is -- True, there is...

00:08:19.965 --> 00:08:22.001
Wait. Hold on.

00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:24.971
Oh, I said you had to cut off
the head to test for rabies.

00:08:24.971 --> 00:08:26.172
And someone said,
"Cutting off the head

00:08:26.172 --> 00:08:27.506
and sending it out to get tested

00:08:27.506 --> 00:08:30.076
is an outdated method
of rabies detection."

00:08:30.076 --> 00:08:33.379
Nowadays, tests are performed on
samples of saliva serum,

00:08:33.379 --> 00:08:36.214
spinal fluid, and skin
biopsies of hair follicles

00:08:36.214 --> 00:08:38.751
at the nape of the neck."

00:08:38.751 --> 00:08:41.721
So it's outdated
to cut off the head.

00:08:43.722 --> 00:08:45.724
So if you ever are getting
tested for rabies

00:08:45.724 --> 00:08:48.327
and your doctor describes
himself as old-fashioned,

00:08:48.327 --> 00:08:49.228
run!

00:08:49.228 --> 00:08:50.863
[ Laughter ]

00:08:53.132 --> 00:08:55.434
We had two jokes
in "Surprise Inspection"

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:58.003
that I did not name
the writer of.

00:08:58.003 --> 00:08:59.605
They were both Scollins'.

00:08:59.605 --> 00:09:01.874
[ Laughter ]

00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:05.244
One was a lawyer
who exposed himself,

00:09:05.244 --> 00:09:08.614
referred to his genitals
as Exhibit D,

00:09:08.614 --> 00:09:13.619
and the other was about
Mac Tonight --

00:09:13.619 --> 00:09:19.224
the the moon-headed
McDonald's mascot...

00:09:19.224 --> 00:09:22.461
doing cocaine.

00:09:22.461 --> 00:09:25.130
If you're ever on the --
if I ever don't mention it,

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:26.699
it might be Scollins.

00:09:30.335 --> 00:09:35.474
Mac Tonight,
very much a...like Fanta,

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:37.576
developed by Pol Pot...

00:09:37.576 --> 00:09:41.046
[ Laughter ]

00:09:41.046 --> 00:09:43.916
Early -- An early mascot
for the Khmer Rouge.

00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:46.919
[ Laughter ]

00:09:50.455 --> 00:09:53.092
Oh, somebody wrote,
"Hey, Seth has Wally" --

00:09:53.092 --> 00:09:55.794
cue-card Wally -- "has Wally
considered becoming an actor?

00:09:55.794 --> 00:09:57.596
His face is very expressive."

00:09:57.596 --> 00:09:58.664
Mm-hmm.

00:09:58.664 --> 00:10:01.467
[ Laughter ]

00:10:08.974 --> 00:10:12.044
"Corrections" is --
Again, this isn't a place

00:10:12.044 --> 00:10:15.080
to brag or toot our own horn,

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:17.683
but it is Emmy-nominated
for its second time

00:10:17.683 --> 00:10:22.688
and against some very fine
competition once again --

00:10:22.688 --> 00:10:25.024
defending champ
"Carpool Karaoke,"

00:10:25.024 --> 00:10:26.626
"Randy Rainbow"...

00:10:28.728 --> 00:10:31.764
...Stephen Colbert's
"Tuning Out the News,"

00:10:31.764 --> 00:10:36.001
and "I Think You Should Leave"
with our friend Tim Robinson.

00:10:36.001 --> 00:10:40.806
So wonderful -- I don't know
who I'm gonna vote for...

00:10:40.806 --> 00:10:42.308
but I'm going to use this time

00:10:42.308 --> 00:10:44.876
in between rounds
to watch them all.

00:10:44.876 --> 00:10:47.613
And I will, at some point,
politic,

00:10:47.613 --> 00:10:49.882
but not necessarily for this

00:10:49.882 --> 00:10:53.018
if I don't feel like
it's the best one.

00:10:53.018 --> 00:10:55.454
Because who am I
if I just throw away my vote?

00:10:58.957 --> 00:11:02.995
We mentioned
Henry Wadsworth Longfellow

00:11:02.995 --> 00:11:04.997
and his poem
"Paul Revere's Ride"

00:11:04.997 --> 00:11:09.435
and how that poem
is historically inaccurate.

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:14.139
And somebody wrote this --
I could not verify

00:11:14.139 --> 00:11:17.876
this fact about
Henry Wadsworth Longfellow,

00:11:17.876 --> 00:11:22.248
but somebody wrote that he wrote
the midnight ride of Paul Revere

00:11:22.248 --> 00:11:25.418
for the most noble of reasons --
a girl.

00:11:25.418 --> 00:11:28.154
He was wooing, a girl whose aunt

00:11:28.154 --> 00:11:33.725
was a Paul Revere descendant
and super into

00:11:33.725 --> 00:11:34.860
that part of her identity.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:37.629
And the story goes
Longfellow wrote the poem

00:11:37.629 --> 00:11:39.565
to get in her good graces

00:11:39.565 --> 00:11:42.534
so he could get
with the niece.

00:11:42.534 --> 00:11:44.436
And if that's true, Henry,

00:11:44.436 --> 00:11:46.205
I hope you got your Wadsworth.

00:11:46.205 --> 00:11:49.542
[ Laughter ]

00:11:53.212 --> 00:11:54.947
We have fun.

00:11:57.282 --> 00:11:58.350
Oh.

00:12:00.186 --> 00:12:01.586
The mugs.

00:12:01.586 --> 00:12:04.723
I got an update today
on the mugs.

00:12:04.723 --> 00:12:08.026
I got some very exciting news
about the mugs...

00:12:08.026 --> 00:12:10.795
and I was told I could make
a very exciting announcement

00:12:10.795 --> 00:12:12.698
about the mugs.

00:12:12.698 --> 00:12:15.000
And then right
before I came out,

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:16.402
someone ran up to me and said,

00:12:16.402 --> 00:12:19.505
"Don't say anything
about the mugs!"

00:12:19.505 --> 00:12:22.140
But I think it's pretty close,
you guys.

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:23.175
And here's the thing.

00:12:23.175 --> 00:12:25.043
I need to remind you again --

00:12:25.043 --> 00:12:28.480
we got to move the mugs.

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:30.116
We got to move the mugs

00:12:30.116 --> 00:12:34.687
'cause I made some promises to
the merch people about the mugs.

00:12:34.687 --> 00:12:37.322
And I'm realizing after having
talked to some of my friends

00:12:37.322 --> 00:12:41.126
who went to business school
that I made a mistake,

00:12:41.126 --> 00:12:43.061
'cause they said, "How many mugs
do you think we'll need?"

00:12:43.061 --> 00:12:44.096
and I said, "I don't know.

00:12:44.096 --> 00:12:46.065
About 300,000 people
watch an episode

00:12:46.065 --> 00:12:47.433
of 'Corrections' each week."

00:12:47.433 --> 00:12:49.134
And they said, "How many do you
think will buy mugs?"

00:12:49.134 --> 00:12:50.903
And I said, "Half?"

00:12:53.972 --> 00:12:55.774
That might be high, guys.

00:12:55.774 --> 00:12:58.377
[ Laughter ]

00:12:58.377 --> 00:13:01.781
So, like, maybe just get one for
you and get one for a friend?

00:13:03.715 --> 00:13:06.951
Anyway, tune in next week for
a hopefully exciting mug news,

00:13:06.951 --> 00:13:09.388
and we'll see you for yet
another "Summer Corrections."

